  "summary": "A distinctive architectural style from Kerala featuring sloping roofs, wood carvings, and courtyard layouts.",
  "description": "Kerala architecture is characterized by steeply sloped roofs covered with terracotta tiles, extensive use of wood, and interconnected buildings around central courtyards. This style evolved to suit Kerala's tropical climate, heavy rainfall, and cultural emphasis on community living and water management.",
  "culturalContext": "Kerala architecture — Thachu-shastra timber tradition under monsoon climate — builds pitched-copper-roofed temples and nalukettu courtyard homes: steep gables shedding the southwest monsoon, carved timber columns and kathirmukham brackets, murals, and naalukettu's four-winged courtyard (nadumuttam) open to rain and light. The style's teak-and-laterite craft survives in Padmanabhapuram Palace and village temples; its low, hipped profile is Kerala's instant identity.",
